  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Anatomy and classification', note: 'Serif (the book voice), sans-serif (the modern voice), mono (the machine voice), script/display (the personality voice). Baseline, x-height, cap height, aperture — the vocabulary that makes type talk legible to you.' },
    { math: '2 · Readability engineering', note: 'x-height (bigger reads smaller at size), measure (45–75 characters per line — the literature lesson\'s line-length wisdom), leading (line spacing: ~120–145% of size), contrast (weights distinguish, sizes rank). Readability is typographic physics.' },
    { math: '3 · Hierarchy: what the eye meets first', note: 'Size, weight, colour and SPACE create ranking — the visual-arts lesson\'s value structure, typographic edition. A page with real hierarchy can be read at a glance before anyone reads a word.' },
    { math: '4 · Micro-typography: the craft layer', note: 'Kerning (pairs: AV, To), tracking (groups: all-caps needs air), ligatures, hanging punctuation, optical alignment (borders fool geometry). The last 5% of polish that separates designed from assembled.' },
  ],
