<TopicPage slug="subjects/design/intermediate/typography-fundamentals">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Typography is <strong>written language made visible</strong> — and the design lesson\'s
    hierarchy principle at its most refined. A page is typography plus space; getting both right
    is most of visual design.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="classification, readability, hierarchy, micro-craft" widget="equationStep" config={typoCfg} />

  <h2>Working knowledge</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Two typefaces are plenty:</strong> a pair (serif + sans, or two weights of one family) with size/weight variation covers 95% of professional needs; every additional voice must argue its case.</li>
    <li><strong>Web fonts changed the engineering:</strong> variable fonts (one file, an axis of weights), system-font stacks for speed, and the design lesson\'s performance budget in kilobytes per headline (the web lesson\'s load discipline).</li>
    <li><strong>Fonts carry register:</strong> Futura = geometric modernism; Garamond = humanist bookishness; Comic Sans = a lesson in context (a friendly font with a reputation earned in the wrong rooms) — the connotation lesson, alphabetic edition.</li>
    <li><strong>Steal from the best documents:</strong> study printed books (500 years of refinement), Swiss posters (hierarchy as art), and the spec sheets of good type foundries — the design education is free and everywhere visible once you can see it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Bringhurst, The Elements of Typographic Style; Lupton, Thinking with Type; Butterick\'s Practical Typography (free online).</p>
</TopicPage>
